<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chera Perumals of Makotai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 800 – <a href="/years/1124">1124</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Later Cheras</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chera Perumals of Makotai were a dynasty that ruled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from about 800 to the early 12th century, with their capital at Makotai, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahodayapuram">Mahodayapuram</a>, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodungallur">Kodungallur</a>. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate grants, such as the Syrian Christian plates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kollam">Kollam</a>, show a kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>, temple councils and active trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>. After the dynasty ended, chiefdoms such as Venad became independent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chera-perumals-of-makotai.jpg" alt="Chera Perumals of Makotai" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sundar Lakshman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_India_in_early_11th_century_AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a> claimed descent from the early historic Cheras of pre-Pallava south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, a lineage tied to the prestige of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangam-literature">Sangam literature</a>, and they are often described as members of the Surya Vamsa, the Solar Race. Present-day central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> probably broke away from a larger Kongu Chera or Kerala kingdom around the 8th or 9th century. At first the dynasty's influence seems to have reached only from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kollam">Kollam</a> to Koyilandy, but it later extended north to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragiri">Chandragiri</a> river and south to Nagercoil.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Most royal revenue came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> spice trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, with Nambudiri-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> settlements on the fertile wetlands as another main support. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sthanu-ravi">Sthanu Ravi</a>, the earliest recorded ruler, helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> conquer the western Tamil country in the late 9th century, but the kingdom was later subdued under the Chola emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-i">Rajaraja I</a>, and firm Chola control followed under Rajendra I. Merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> such as manigramam and anjuvannam were active, West Asian Muslims settled as traders, and the earliest inscriptional Malayalam is tentatively dated to this period.</p><p>In the early 12th century the kingdom dissolved into local powers, a collapse in which the gradual change of land relations is thought to have played a crucial part. Besides Venad in the south, the chieftains of Kolathunad and the future rulers of Kozhikode and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Kochi</a> emerged as successors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> historiography called the state a centralised Second Chera Empire or Kulasekhara Empire. Research of the late 1960s and early 1970s rejected this, finding that only two kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sthanu-ravi">Sthanu Ravi Kulasekhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-kulashekhara">Rama Kulasekhara</a>, are known to have used that regnal name. Some historians since the early 2000s, such as M. G. S. Narayanan, see the king's power as largely ritual and confined to the capital, with real power held by local chieftains, the udaiyavar, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> oligarchy.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>, or nadus, owed the king service in war and dues collected by a royal prince, the Koyil Adhikarikal. The managers of four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> temples around the capital, the Nalu Thali, likely served as his council, and the Ayiram, a thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nair">Nair</a> warriors, formed his guard.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chera Perumals of Kerala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
